WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.810
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:09.026
In this video, we're going to be talking about how to automate testing for your Python project.

00:00:09.026 --> 00:00:13.846
The goal of this video is to show you what you would have to do to take your project

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:17.855
that might be something simple like this, just in a Github repo somewhere

00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:20.900
and you have maybe a single file with some code in it

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.900
and

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.382
maybe you have some tests already, maybe you don't

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:28.226
but they're not automatically running every time you commit.

00:00:28.226 --> 00:00:31.973
So, we're going to take a repo like this that's very bare-bones.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:34.988
and turn it into something more like this.

00:00:34.988 --> 00:00:37.693
This repo has a specific structure to it.

00:00:37.693 --> 00:00:42.077
And as you can see, I've got a little badge down here telling me that my tests are passing.

00:00:42.077 --> 00:00:45.923
And it's not just a badge. It actually runs the tests.

00:00:45.923 --> 00:00:50.958
So, I can click on this checkmark here and we can see the details of the last test that ran.

00:00:50.958 --> 00:00:55.377
And you can see that the tests have run across multiple different operating systems.

00:00:55.377 --> 00:01:00.684
I have Ubuntu and Windows and across multiple different versions of Python.

00:01:00.684 --> 00:01:05.379
For each one of these tests, we essentially have an isolated environment

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.979
that checks out all of our code and runs all of our tests in a way

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:15.596
that would be similar to what someone would see if they were to use your project in a fresh virtual environment.

00:01:15.596 --> 00:01:21.534
Moreover, all of these tests run automatically every time I push a commit to my repository.

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:25.415
So, here's the overview of what we're going to go over.

00:01:25.415 --> 00:01:32.134
First, we're going to go over how to either set up or restructure your project in order to make it easily testable

00:01:32.134 --> 00:01:35.062
including all of these different configuration files

00:01:35.062 --> 00:01:37.619
what do they all mean and what are they used for?

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:40.685
Second, we're going to learn how to use pytest for running your tests,

00:01:40.685 --> 00:01:47.132
mypy for checking type hints and flake8 for linting or checking code style.

00:01:47.132 --> 00:01:50.185
Third, we're going to learn about tox and how we can use tox

00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:54.967
in order to run all of your tests in multiple different isolated Python environments.

00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:58.841
Finally, we're going to learn how to use Github actions in order to run tox

00:01:58.841 --> 00:02:03.440
and run all of our tests every time that we push to our code repository.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.737
Now, here are a few things that I'm not going to talk about.

00:02:06.737 --> 00:02:09.714
First off, testing is a huge topic.

00:02:09.714 --> 00:02:16.387
And it's under the umbrella of continuous integration and continuous delivery or continuous deployment.

00:02:16.387 --> 00:02:20.738
These are very very broad topics and I'm not going to cover them in general.

00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:23.759
I just want to focus on automated testing.

00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:27.895
I'm not going to be talking about publishing your package to PyPi

00:02:27.895 --> 00:02:35.320
or automatically building documentation, automatically formatting your code on git commits or any other pre-commit hooks.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.708
I'm also not going to talk about how to generate downloadable artifacts like coverage reports.

00:02:39.708 --> 00:02:44.847
And I'm not going to talk about more complicated builds, like if you're building a C extension.

00:02:44.847 --> 00:02:51.703
I just want to focus on testing alone because I think that once you go from no automation to adding automated tests

00:02:51.703 --> 00:02:54.437
that's sort of the biggest leap that you have to make.

00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:57.034
Okay, with that in mind, let's just dive into it.

00:02:57.034 --> 00:02:59.333
First up, Project Structure.

00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:02.351
We're here in PyCharm and I've already cloned the repository.

00:03:02.351 --> 00:03:07.040
You can see, we have a very flat structure and this is the project that we're working on.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.301
Basically, it's called slap that like button.

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:17.035
and what it is is a model of what happens when you hit the like or dislike buttons on youtube.

00:03:17.035 --> 00:03:18.500
So, we have three states.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.360
It can either be a liked video, a dislike video or nothing, an empty like state.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.918
And then we just have some functions.

00:03:25.918 --> 00:03:28.640
And then I have some tests for what happens

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.450
if you start it empty and then hit the like or dislike button some number of times.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.819
Well, for once the code that we've got here is actually pretty irrelevant to what we're going to be doing.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:42.553
You can replace this with one of your own files and it's not going to make much difference.

00:03:42.553 --> 00:03:46.780
Most of the work that we're going to be doing is just in the setup and configuration.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.684
So, don't worry about trying to, you know, pause and like look at what all these functions do. It really doesn't matter.

00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:56.046
The first thing that I'm going to do is take any tests that you do have

00:03:56.046 --> 00:04:00.000
and separate them out from the actual library code or application code.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.490
Our tests are going to be in a completely separate place from the rest of the code.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:11.120
Okay. So, I've just moved the test to a separate file called test_slapping.py in the same directory for now.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.185
and imported everything that I need in order to run the test.

00:04:14.185 --> 00:04:18.275
So, right now I'm actually depending on the fact that the test is right next to the module

00:04:18.275 --> 00:04:22.315
that it's testing so that I can import it just by using the name.

00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:26.015
It just so happens that because they're right next to each other this import works.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:28.948
For automated testing, I don't want to depend on that behavior.

00:04:28.948 --> 00:04:31.929
My tests should run no matter where they are.

00:04:31.929 --> 00:04:35.969
The way to make that happen is to make your project an installable package.

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:41.038
So, I went ahead and created a source directory containing a package called slapping.

00:04:41.038 --> 00:04:45.019
Slapping is going to be the name of this slap_that_like_button package, okay?

00:04:45.019 --> 00:04:48.369
So, here we have the slap_that_like_button file that we had before

00:04:48.369 --> 00:04:53.620
and then we now have a separate test directory where I put the test_slapping.py file into it.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.032
Now that the things are separated, you can see that my editor is warning me that

00:04:57.032 --> 00:04:59.657
it doesn't know how to import slap_that_like_button.

00:04:59.657 --> 00:05:04.269
So, our package is called slapping. So, eventually it should look like this.

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:07.529
But currently this package is not installable.

00:05:07.529 --> 00:05:08.977
So, we need to fix that.

00:05:08.977 --> 00:05:11.393
In order to make the package instalable in a consistent way,

00:05:11.393 --> 00:05:14.577
we're going to have to add a bunch of configuration files.

00:05:14.577 --> 00:05:17.734
The first of which is this pyproject.toml.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.335
Now, this is kind of a touchy subject for the Python community.

00:05:21.335 --> 00:05:27.289
There really shouldn't be any reason that I need eight different configuration files in order to make a package.

00:05:27.289 --> 00:05:30.765
But that's kind of the state of affairs right now.

00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:36.494
There's no reason that you should be expected to know what these files are or what things go where.

00:05:36.494 --> 00:05:41.509
Basically, you should just find a project that you already know and trust. And copy what they're doing.

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:44.349
Okay, first up, the pyproject.toml.

00:05:44.349 --> 00:05:48.138
Basically, in the olden days there was only one way to install packages.

00:05:48.138 --> 00:05:50.539
You needed this setup.py file.

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:53.076
But nowadays, there are many different alternatives.

00:05:53.076 --> 00:05:57.202
You can use something like poetry or flit. Or you can still use setup tools.

00:05:57.202 --> 00:06:02.009
pyproject.toml just tells us that we're still using the old way.

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:03.855
Next up is, setup.py

00:06:03.855 --> 00:06:07.761
setup.py used to be the place where you put your installation script.

00:06:07.761 --> 00:06:11.377
It would do everything that you needed to do in order to install the Python package.

00:06:11.377 --> 00:06:17.020
So, you can run arbitrary code inside of your setup.py file since it is a Python script.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:20.307
And this is seen increasingly as a security risk.

00:06:20.307 --> 00:06:24.666
And so more and more of the code is being stripped out of the setup.py file

00:06:24.666 --> 00:06:28.231
and put into one of these other configuration files.

00:06:28.231 --> 00:06:29.914
Even though it's basically empty,

00:06:29.914 --> 00:06:36.232
this exact file is what's going to allow us to install our package in editable mode which will be important later on.

00:06:36.232 --> 00:06:40.999
So then, where do I store all the metadata of my project like the title and description?

00:06:40.999 --> 00:06:45.424
Well, all that stuff now goes in the setup.cfg file.

00:06:45.424 --> 00:06:48.535
Since this is just a configuration file and not a Python script,

00:06:48.535 --> 00:06:51.681
you don't have to worry about it executing arbitrary code.

00:06:51.681 --> 00:06:56.059
Here, you can see basic metadata, like the name, description, author, that kind of thing.

00:06:56.059 --> 00:07:00.154
Then down here, we have some information about what packages we're trying to package up.

00:07:00.154 --> 00:07:03.098
So, in this case we're packaging up the slapping library.

00:07:03.098 --> 00:07:07.744
And to install this library, we're just going to pretend that it depends on the request library.

00:07:07.744 --> 00:07:09.971
I don't actually use requests in this repository.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:13.841
I'm just showing you so that you know this is the place where you would put those dependencies.

00:07:13.841 --> 00:07:19.559
Another important option here is that we're saying that our packages are contained in this source directory.

00:07:19.559 --> 00:07:23.793
Then I go ahead and make a requirements.txt file that has all of my dependencies.

00:07:23.793 --> 00:07:29.292
In this case, just requests and it's good practice to give very specific version numbers.

00:07:29.292 --> 00:07:33.118
So, in the setup I just said requests bigger than or equal to two.

00:07:33.118 --> 00:07:36.651
And in this file, I'm giving a specific version.

00:07:36.651 --> 00:07:42.801
So, at this point, all of this setup and configuration has led up to being able to install the package.

00:07:42.801 --> 00:07:44.014
So, here's what I can do.

00:07:44.014 --> 00:07:50.737
I say pip install -e current directory.

00:07:50.737 --> 00:07:55.229
It went ahead and downloaded all the dependencies, namely the request library

00:07:55.229 --> 00:07:59.569
and it installed the slapping library into the current virtual environment.

00:07:59.569 --> 00:08:03.153
If you're wondering about this dash e and what editable mode actually means,

00:08:03.153 --> 00:08:08.931
all it does is that in the virtual environment, it just puts a link to the actual source code directory.

00:08:08.931 --> 00:08:14.832
That way, if I make a change to like the slap that like button file, I don't have to reinstall the slapping package.

00:08:14.832 --> 00:08:20.088
You can see now that when I go back to my test file, I no longer have a red squiggly line under the slapping library.

00:08:20.088 --> 00:08:23.258
PyCharm knows that slapping is an installed package

00:08:23.258 --> 00:08:28.794
and it knows how to import this regardless of the fact that these two are in completely separate directories.

00:08:28.794 --> 00:08:31.945
Again, we're able to do this because we made our project into a package

00:08:31.945 --> 00:08:35.509
and then installed that package into the current environment.

00:08:35.509 --> 00:08:38.899
Okay, now we can get to the libraries that actually do the testing.

00:08:38.899 --> 00:08:43.927
I'm talking about pytest, mypy and flake8 for the purposes of this video.

00:08:43.927 --> 00:08:47.305
That means, you guessed it, more configuration files.

00:08:47.305 --> 00:08:49.589
First up, I made another requirements file.

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:52.054
This one with just the development requirements.

00:08:52.054 --> 00:08:55.630
And those are kind of separate from the normal requirements of the project.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:59.852
Because if you just want to use this, you would only need the request library.

00:08:59.852 --> 00:09:03.038
But if you want to run the tests, then you need all this other stuff.

00:09:03.038 --> 00:09:05.389
That's why I put them in different files.

00:09:05.389 --> 00:09:09.518
So, I just added these lines to the setup.cfg file.

00:09:09.518 --> 00:09:14.678
This section is how you indicate that a Python package has been type hinted.

00:09:14.678 --> 00:09:18.630
So, the slapping package is type hinted.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:24.247
This py.typed needs to be just a blank file right next to the init of the package.

00:09:24.247 --> 00:09:28.953
Then here, we have some configuration for the flake8 program that we're going to be using.

00:09:28.953 --> 00:09:34.373
So, this is kind of annoying. But some programs want their configuration in the cfg file,

00:09:34.373 --> 00:09:37.514
some programs want it in the toml file,

00:09:37.514 --> 00:09:39.920
some programs want their own configuration file.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.000
And there's no one right answer.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.752
Some allow both, some prefer one over the other.

00:09:44.752 --> 00:09:50.262
For each program, you just have to look at its documentation and see where you can put its configuration.

00:09:50.262 --> 00:09:55.686
I think the community is moving to pushing more and more of the configuration into the toml file

00:09:55.686 --> 00:09:59.078
and then just leaving metadata in the cfg file.

00:09:59.078 --> 00:10:02.096
But for now, this is just the state of affairs.

00:10:02.096 --> 00:10:04.890
So, our flake8 configuration is going to be here.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:09.935
But our configuration for pytest and mypy is going to be in the toml file.

00:10:09.935 --> 00:10:13.488
So, I'll just go to the toml file and then paste those in.

00:10:13.488 --> 00:10:16.774
I'm not going to go over all the configuration options and what they mean.

00:10:16.774 --> 00:10:18.929
I think they're pretty self-explanatory.

00:10:18.929 --> 00:10:22.748
Of course, we just added some dev dependencies to our project like pytest.

00:10:22.748 --> 00:10:28.296
So, we do need to go ahead and install everything from the requirements.txt

00:10:28.296 --> 00:10:34.210
Now that we have those installed, we can go ahead and run mypi on our source directory and see that we have no issues.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:38.904
We can run our linter on our source directory and see that we have no issues.

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:44.528
And we can run pytest and we already told pytest and its configuration where the test is.

00:10:44.528 --> 00:10:47.567
So, with mypy and flake8, they're pretty self-explanatory.

00:10:47.567 --> 00:10:50.544
You just run them and they check your code and tell you if something's wrong.

00:10:50.544 --> 00:10:53.306
But with pytest, there's actually a little bit more to it.

00:10:53.306 --> 00:10:58.187
Namely, you have to know how to use it. It's a library and it helps you write tests.

00:10:58.187 --> 00:11:03.791
So, let's just go through some of the basic principles of how pytest works and what are its features.

00:11:03.791 --> 00:11:09.180
Essentially, it just looks through your tests directory which we specified in the configuration.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:12.837
And looks for any module that starts with test underscore.

00:11:12.837 --> 00:11:17.828
And then within those modules, it looks for any function that starts with test underscore.

00:11:17.828 --> 00:11:21.025
And then it assumes that those are tests and runs them.

00:11:21.025 --> 00:11:23.497
In this case, this is the only test that it found.

00:11:23.497 --> 00:11:28.075
But we actually have a bunch of different test cases in this one test function.

00:11:28.075 --> 00:11:32.629
So, I split it up into three separate functions based off of the behavior that they're testing.

00:11:32.629 --> 00:11:36.916
Even though these are all morally testing, what happens when there are many slaps happening.

00:11:36.916 --> 00:11:41.480
There's still a lot of different cases that are going on in this one function.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:46.480
If this first case were to fail, this test wouldn't run any of the rest of them.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.219
For these cases, pytest allows you to parametrize your tests.

00:11:50.219 --> 00:11:57.600
This mark parametrized decorator allows you to feed a bunch of different test cases into a single test function.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.876
So, I specify what are the arguments that I'm going to be passing in,

00:12:00.876 --> 00:12:04.103
in this case the input and the expected answer

00:12:04.103 --> 00:12:09.410
and then those values that I pass in appear as the arguments in the function.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:12.000
So, I'm going to have a test input of ll.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.515
So, if you slap the like button twice,

00:12:14.515 --> 00:12:20.008
then the expected answer should be that it ends up with the empty state.

00:12:20.008 --> 00:12:25.576
You pass in a list of tuples that have all the different pairs of test input and expected.

00:12:25.576 --> 00:12:29.160
And then each one of those becomes its own test.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:32.151
So, with this setup, it's the same tests as before.

00:12:32.151 --> 00:12:35.699
But if one of them fails, it's still going to run all the rest of them.

00:12:35.699 --> 00:12:39.072
You can also use this decorator to skip a test.

00:12:39.072 --> 00:12:43.362
For instance, here I have a test for a regex slap pattern

00:12:43.362 --> 00:12:49.816
where if you do any sequence of likes and dislikes followed by two dislikes and a like,

00:12:49.816 --> 00:12:52.334
then you end up in the like state.

00:12:52.334 --> 00:12:56.690
But of course, I haven't implemented this feature. So, I'm just going to skip this test.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:04.410
You can also use a related function skip if to skip tests based off of things like is the operating system Windows versus Mac.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:09.638
If you have a test that you know is failing and just for some reason don't want it to fail the build,

00:13:09.638 --> 00:13:13.260
then you can mark it with this xfail, expected fail,

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:20.423
then it'll still run the test but it won't count as a failure for the purposes of did the build complete or not.

00:13:20.423 --> 00:13:25.380
However, an expected fail is not what you should use if you're expecting an exception.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:31.507
If I wanted to test whether I get a specific exception, there's a separate mechanism for doing that.

00:13:31.507 --> 00:13:34.869
So here, we have a test case where we're testing an invalid slap.

00:13:34.869 --> 00:13:39.618
The valid slaps are like and dislike. You can slap the like button or the dislike button.

00:13:39.618 --> 00:13:42.133
But here I'm trying to x.

00:13:42.133 --> 00:13:48.130
The way to tell pytest that you are expecting a specific exception is by using a with statement

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:52.120
and saying pytest raises and then the name of the error that you're expecting.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:56.539
This test will now fail if a value error is not raised.

00:13:56.539 --> 00:14:00.895
The last extremely important feature of pytest is fixtures.

00:14:00.895 --> 00:14:07.150
Fixtures are what you use if a bunch of tests require a certain amount of setup that they all share in common.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:10.974
Imagine that, you have a bunch of tests that require a database connection.

00:14:10.974 --> 00:14:16.410
It wouldn't be good practice to copy and paste the setup code for the database connection across all of the tests.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:19.630
Instead, you can use a fixture that gets passed to your test.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:24.983
By convention, if you create a file named conftest.py and create a fixture in that file,

00:14:24.983 --> 00:14:27.406
it'll be available for all of your tests.

00:14:27.406 --> 00:14:29.950
You can create fixtures just right next to the tests.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:33.900
But if you want them to be available everywhere then put them in conftest.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:39.221
Fixtures are good because they allow you to avoid a lot of boilerplate setup and tear down code for tests.

00:14:39.221 --> 00:14:41.233
And also, they can have different scopes.

00:14:41.233 --> 00:14:46.138
So, here I use the fixture decorator which marks this db_conn function as a fixture.

00:14:46.138 --> 00:14:51.528
What that means is, if I have a test function that has db_conn as an argument name,

00:14:51.528 --> 00:14:56.427
such as this one, then what it's going to do is call this function

00:14:56.427 --> 00:14:59.245
and depending on whether it returns or yields something,

00:14:59.245 --> 00:15:04.678
it will take the either returned or yielded value and put that in as the argument to the function.

00:15:04.678 --> 00:15:08.236
So, in this case we can create a database url and then create a connection

00:15:08.236 --> 00:15:11.656
and then yield that connection to any test that wants it.

00:15:11.656 --> 00:15:16.431
You should use yield instead of return if you have anything that requires tear-down code.

00:15:16.431 --> 00:15:20.563
In this case, the connection presumably needs to be closed after all the tests are done

00:15:20.563 --> 00:15:24.218
and that's going to happen at the end of the width block.

00:15:24.218 --> 00:15:26.882
So, in this file, where I have db_conn as an argument,

00:15:26.882 --> 00:15:30.150
what's going to happen is it's going to call this create the connection

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:37.320
and then pass it as an argument here to this function or any other function that has this db con as an argument.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:43.270
The default behavior for a fixture is that it will run the fixture code for every single test.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:46.920
But if you have something very expensive to create like a database connection,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.990
then you might want to share that same database connection amongst all the tests.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.156
The way you can do that is by changing the scope.

00:15:54.156 --> 00:15:58.408
So, if you say scope equals session, then it will only run this function once

00:15:58.408 --> 00:16:03.776
and then cache the value and pass the same database connection to all of your different tests.

00:16:03.776 --> 00:16:06.677
Fixtures can also depend on other fixtures.

00:16:06.677 --> 00:16:13.670
In this case, I'm making a fixture called capture_std_out that depends on a built-in fixture called monkeypatch.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:19.550
Suppose that, I want to test something like something gets printed to standard out when I run this function.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:26.478
Well, if I actually called the function and it printed to standard out, that's gone. I have no handle on that.

00:16:26.478 --> 00:16:31.540
A way I could get around this is by changing what the system standard out write function does.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:37.230
What if instead of actually writing to standard out, I had to just append to a string in a buffer.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:38.815
Well, then my test would be really easy.

00:16:38.815 --> 00:16:43.202
I could just pass the buffer around and check to see the value of the buffer at the end of the test.

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:50.922
Now, what monkeypatch allows me to do is change the right attribute of the real system.stdout to my fake write function.

00:16:50.922 --> 00:16:54.975
What makes monkeypatch so convenient is that everything is automatically undone

00:16:54.975 --> 00:16:57.890
and put back the way that it was before at the end of the test.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:02.728
So, I haven't just messed up my standard out and pytest itself can still print say the test results.

00:17:02.728 --> 00:17:05.515
Then I could use my capture_stdout fixture like this.

00:17:05.515 --> 00:17:08.589
So, I make an argument with that name to my test function.

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:11.739
So, it runs all of the code to monkeypatch standard out.

00:17:11.739 --> 00:17:16.777
Then I just call something that I know prints to standard out like the print function

00:17:16.777 --> 00:17:19.568
and then I go ahead and check what's in the buffer.

00:17:19.568 --> 00:17:24.565
So, fixtures can be extremely useful for setting up before test and tearing down after a test.

00:17:24.565 --> 00:17:30.393
Test can depend on fixtures and fixtures can depend on fixtures and there are a lot of really useful ones already built in.

00:17:30.393 --> 00:17:36.711
So, at this point, I can run pytest and see that I get all of my passing tests one skipped and two expected failures.

00:17:36.711 --> 00:17:39.682
One thing I haven't mentioned yet is this down here.

00:17:39.682 --> 00:17:41.750
This is called the coverage report.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.734
This is printed out because we put a --cov in the configuration.

00:17:45.734 --> 00:17:49.309
You can see that my tests cover 100% of the source code.

00:17:49.309 --> 00:17:55.685
What this means is that all of my tests combined touched every single line of executable code in my source.

00:17:55.685 --> 00:18:00.760
If this number is less than 100, then that means some part of your code is untested.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:03.960
Of course, that doesn't make it wrong and that doesn't mean that there is a bug.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:09.722
And having 100 test coverage doesn't mean that there's not a bug but it's just an indicator.

00:18:09.722 --> 00:18:14.312
If you look into the pytest coverage options, you can even get a printout with html

00:18:14.312 --> 00:18:17.694
and it'll show you exactly which lines are hit and which lines aren't.

00:18:17.694 --> 00:18:21.905
So, suppose that, at this point, all of our tests are running.

00:18:21.905 --> 00:18:26.990
mypy passes, pytest passes, flake8 passes. Everything is good.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:31.381
And everything essentially is good in our current virtual environment.

00:18:31.381 --> 00:18:34.410
That's what just running those commands outright does.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:42.580
But, how do we know that everything is still going to work in a fresh environment or using a different version of Python?

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:45.910
Of course, to do all this, we need yet another configuration file.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.714
This one is called tox.ini

00:18:48.714 --> 00:18:53.054
Tox allows you to create a bunch of different virtual environments

00:18:53.054 --> 00:18:58.424
that are completely fresh and install your package into those virtual environments

00:18:58.424 --> 00:19:04.979
and then automatically run your tests or mypy or whatever else you want in that new fresh environment.

00:19:04.979 --> 00:19:09.456
These first four are just built-in versions of Python that tox already knows about.

00:19:09.456 --> 00:19:13.490
Their configuration goes in this test end block here.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:19.169
So, you see, I installed the dev requirements and then run a pyest command in any of those situations.

00:19:19.169 --> 00:19:23.170
These last two environments are not built into tox. You can define your own.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:26.559
So here, I define my own flake8 and mypy environments.

00:19:26.559 --> 00:19:29.106
And their configuration goes in these blocks.

00:19:29.106 --> 00:19:33.690
Since flake8 is not a version of Python, it's just a command that I want to run,

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:38.049
I still have to specify what version of Python is the base environment in.

00:19:38.049 --> 00:19:43.825
So, let's just say that I'm going to be running my mypy and flake8 in a Python 3.6 environment.

00:19:43.825 --> 00:19:47.846
Then I just install whatever dependencies and then run the command that I want to run.

00:19:47.846 --> 00:19:51.090
Once I have this configuration and pip install tox,

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:56.380
then it's as simple as just running the tox command with no arguments.

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:59.118
You can see right now. It's creating the package.

00:19:59.118 --> 00:20:02.529
It's installing setup tools and wheel.

00:20:02.529 --> 00:20:04.855
It's creating the py36 environment.

00:20:04.855 --> 00:20:08.168
It's installing the dependencies for my project in that environment.

00:20:08.168 --> 00:20:10.650
And then, it's going to go ahead and run the tests.

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:13.660
I'll go ahead and probably speed through this portion.

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:18.015
Okay. And it finally finished and as you can see everything passed.

00:20:18.015 --> 00:20:21.775
so py36, py37, py38, py39, flake8 and mypy everything passed.

00:20:21.775 --> 00:20:23.513
So, we get a nice little smiley face.

00:20:23.513 --> 00:20:29.798
But if there's one thing to take away from this, is that that took a long time.

00:20:29.798 --> 00:20:35.030
That took way longer than just running any of the tests individually. So, why did it take so long?

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:38.323
Well the reason is because, when I just run pytest by itself,

00:20:38.323 --> 00:20:41.031
I already have my virtual environment installed.

00:20:41.031 --> 00:20:46.405
But when I run tox, it's going to create a new virtual environment and then install everything into that.

00:20:46.405 --> 00:20:49.723
It's going to download from pip. It's going to install everything and do it that way.

00:20:49.723 --> 00:20:55.390
So, it could take much much longer to run through tox than if you just run pytest by itself.

00:20:55.390 --> 00:21:00.890
In my own development cycle, I run pytest all the time. That's very quick and very easy.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:04.157
It's only when I'm thinking about getting ready to commit and push,

00:21:04.157 --> 00:21:07.380
that I will run tox locally on my own machine.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:12.030
If everything passes in all the different environments, then it's probably safe to commit and push.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:16.290
So, tox allows me to run all of my tests in a bunch of different environments.

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.663
But it's all still just on my own machine.

00:21:18.663 --> 00:21:23.910
How do I gain confidence that these tests are still going to pass on someone else's machine?

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:27.834
This is finally where the automated part of automated testing comes in.

00:21:27.834 --> 00:21:34.830
I'm going to use Github actions to automatically run tox every time I push to my main branch.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:40.092
The way that you configure Github actions is with, you guessed it, another configuration file.

00:21:40.092 --> 00:21:44.347
You need to create a .github folder in your project at the top level

00:21:44.347 --> 00:21:48.011
and then a workflows folder within the .github folder

00:21:48.011 --> 00:21:51.429
and then a yml file within the workflows folder.

00:21:51.429 --> 00:21:56.726
You can have many different files with many different actions. We're just going to put all of our testing stuff in one.

00:21:56.726 --> 00:21:58.798
Give the workflow a good name.

00:21:58.798 --> 00:22:02.697
This is the name that's going to show up in the badge that we put in the readme.

00:22:02.697 --> 00:22:08.178
Here, we're using on to specify that we want this workflow to run every time there's a push or a pull request.

00:22:08.178 --> 00:22:12.438
This workflow is just going to have one job that runs. The test job.

00:22:12.438 --> 00:22:19.489
We use this matrix strategy syntax to basically create all the different combinations of environments that we want to use.

00:22:19.489 --> 00:22:23.826
So, we have strategy, matrix. Under os, we'll have ubuntu and windows

00:22:23.826 --> 00:22:27.755
and Python version will have 3.6, 3.7, 3.8, 3.9.

00:22:27.755 --> 00:22:30.305
Now, all of these things are pre-configured Github options.

00:22:30.305 --> 00:22:37.590
I can't just put in ubuntu 1.0.0 or something like that. It needs to be something that's specifically supported by Github.

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:41.569
You can find a link to all the supported options in the Github actions documentation.

00:22:41.569 --> 00:22:45.568
But most likely, you should just be able to copy paste from this project to get what you want.

00:22:45.568 --> 00:22:49.948
Note here that, the matrix strategy tries every combination of each of these things.

00:22:49.948 --> 00:22:55.793
So, I have two os's and four Python versions that means this is going to create eight test runs.

00:22:55.793 --> 00:22:58.698
Then you just define what are all the steps of your workflow.

00:22:58.698 --> 00:23:01.058
So, we check out our repository.

00:23:01.058 --> 00:23:07.089
Then we set up the version of Python with the given Python version from the matrix.

00:23:07.089 --> 00:23:14.860
Then we install all the dependencies. So, we upgrade pip and then pip install tox and this tox-gh-actions.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:16.920
Then we run tox.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:21.586
Now I haven't said anything about tox-gh-actions before. Let me go over that.

00:23:21.586 --> 00:23:27.518
Basically, Github already provides its own set of actions that sort of mimic what tox does.

00:23:27.518 --> 00:23:32.426
And that would be fine if all you wanted to do was run your tests when you push to Github.

00:23:32.426 --> 00:23:36.198
But if we want to run our tests locally, then we kind of require tox.

00:23:36.198 --> 00:23:38.932
Because we can't run Github actions locally.

00:23:38.932 --> 00:23:42.156
So, someone created this talks-gh-actions package

00:23:42.156 --> 00:23:47.857
which basically makes Github actions and tox work together as well as they possibly could.

00:23:47.857 --> 00:23:54.000
Basically, you just have to give Github away to correspond your tox environments with the Github environments.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:59.137
So, we go back to our tox.ini file and then add this section specific to Github actions.

00:23:59.137 --> 00:24:06.431
All you have to do is list out the names of the Github Python version and what tox environments that corresponds to.

00:24:06.431 --> 00:24:12.450
So, for Github 3.6, we want to run our py36 and our flake8 and mypy.

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:17.758
And 3.7, we just do the py37. 3.8 py38. 3.9 py39.

00:24:17.758 --> 00:24:21.559
And hopefully, if I haven't made any typos, then everything should be set up.

00:24:21.559 --> 00:24:24.329
Let's go ahead and commit our code and try a push.

00:24:24.329 --> 00:24:30.799
So, here I am at the Github repository and now you'll see this little orange dot.

00:24:30.799 --> 00:24:34.730
This little orange dot means that our actions are running.

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:39.290
So, if I click on that, I see all these actions. Let's go into the details.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.285
As you can see, all the tests are running.

00:24:41.285 --> 00:24:45.691
In fact, it appears that they're all running concurrently which is good. It's not going to take as long.

00:24:45.691 --> 00:24:50.205
It's running the test with tox. It's going through the flak 8.

00:24:50.205 --> 00:24:53.040
And a few of them have already started passing.

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:57.135
So, everything looks like it's running correctly. Let's just examine some of the output.

00:24:57.135 --> 00:24:58.842
Let's go into the tox.

00:24:58.842 --> 00:25:04.862
We see the same thing. 14 items, 11 passed, one skips, two expected fails.

00:25:04.862 --> 00:25:08.909
And it looks like everything is passed just the same as it passed locally on my machine.

00:25:08.909 --> 00:25:16.067
So, now I can have confidence that all this stuff works in Ubuntu and Windows and all these different versions of Python.

00:25:16.067 --> 00:25:22.232
Back to the main page of the repository, I now see the check mark that the last set of tests has completed successfully.

00:25:22.232 --> 00:25:27.231
But if I look at the readme, there's no badge here telling everyone how cool I am.

00:25:27.231 --> 00:25:29.570
So, let's see how to do that.

00:25:29.570 --> 00:25:33.079
So, here I am in the readme and this is all the code that you need to add

00:25:33.079 --> 00:25:36.969
in order to add a little badge with the check mark that tells you that your build is passing.

00:25:36.969 --> 00:25:41.990
Basically, Github just provides everything for you. You just need to navigate to the correct link.

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:46.035
So, we have github.com your username or organization or whatever

00:25:46.035 --> 00:25:49.074
then the project name, then actions workflows

00:25:49.074 --> 00:25:52.662
and then the name of the yml file that you used and then badge.svg

00:25:52.662 --> 00:25:56.906
So, the workflow just finished and now you can see that we have this test passing badge.

00:25:56.906 --> 00:26:02.110
Just for fun, let's go break a test and then see what it looks like when it's broken.

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:07.860
So, we'll get rid of the expected failure for the divide_by_zero and then that should definitely cause a failing case.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:11.772
So now, everything completed and we can see that we had three failures 

00:26:11.772 --> 00:26:15.232
and then Github will just cancel them if it notices that they're all failing.

00:26:15.232 --> 00:26:17.778
So, three of them failed and then it canceled the rest.

00:26:17.778 --> 00:26:21.830
And we can just go into the logs and we can see the zero division error.

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:28.750
Now when we're on the front of the page, we see the red x showing that it's failed and we have a test failing here.

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:30.231
And that's all there is to it.

00:26:30.231 --> 00:26:34.934
Feel free to look at this repository yourself. The link is in the description.

